inancial return of just taking a person  off a medication  because every time we take somebody off  a man we probably need to replace it  with something else  now what we actually see in real life is  that over time  people start being on fewer medications  once their meds  are are handled better  and those are drugs that might be  dealing with those side effects  over the course of time when we did our  first  in our initial evaluation we realized  that  there was a huge impact of  pharmacogenomics on this population  in fact about 64 of the medication  action plans resulted in recommended  changes to plan  so out of all of these members who  enrolled the 9  000 of them even when we were looking  several months ago  a large number of them were benefiting  from  having a review by a pharmacist  in fact the top five most frequently  removed drugs  were removed specifically because  genetic concerns and this is dictated  and noted uh by the pharmacists in  the clinical decision support tool  itself  and so quickly i want to talk about the  outcome of this program and first of all  is the reduction in these medication  these ma3 related risk incidents so i  talked earlier about the ma3 incidence  and these are the cpt  codes or rather the icd-10 codes and the  indications the diseases and the things  that happen  the the events and the incidents that  happen to folks  that we know are impacted by  pharmacogenomics  and through our evaluation we saw a  downward trend  in the utilization of the health care  system following map delivery  over the course of the time afterwards  and that was in 13 there was a 13  reduction in those ma3 events  so hugely impactful  even more exciting to the the system  is understanding that cost reduction so  folks that had been  enrolled since january of 2018  were coming on three years for some of  these folks as they rolled  out the program and after the maps were  delivered we saw a 14  reduction in the cost to plan spending  over the  16 months following map delivery  now what's interesting and probably not  surprising to most of you  our internal control group this is  people  in the system that had not gone through  pharmacogenomic testing  they had not received a medication  action plan their physicians and  pharmacists were not involved  they actually had an increase of about  3.2 percent  in that cost to spending  the other metrics of success that we saw  in this program  are wrapped around the number of  recommendations accepted by prescribers  right these are naive prescribers so our  ability to communicate  uh changes in the plan well and  including pharmacists  that number is really impressive early  on in these programs  one might imagine that if you just have  pharmacogenomics  the recommendations aren't always  accepted by prescribers but we've seen  that number go  up over time and by including the other  risk factors  and by including good communication  between the pharmacists  in our team and the prescribing  physicians  we've also seen a reduction 11 percent  specifically in pharmacy spend okay  so you've got all of that total health  care costs have gone down the pharmacy  spend has gone down  and we've also seen a reduction in  hospitalizations  we've seen a reduction in emergency  department visits  and we've seen a 27 percent reduction in  slip and falls  i'd like to think that everybody here  knows somebody who's affected  by medications that just don't work  and wouldn't you like to help those  folks  when those medications don't work or if  they're harmful  knowing that there's ways to do it using  genetics and making that a complete  story using these other risk factors  that are impactful  when considering the health and wellness  of our  society thank you so much for your time  and i look forward to hearing your  questions  after this
 

